of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms ${a}_{10}=30$ and ${a}_{11}=33$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{11}-{a}_{10} \\\\  & =33-30 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{10}=30,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{10}$ is $9$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{10} & ={a}_{1}+9d \\\\ 30 & ={a}_{1}+9(3) \\\\ 30 & ={a}_{1}+27 \\\\ 30-27 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =3+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =3+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23171184-edaf-560e-a5a6-fd7f0be2f6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Consecutive Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d577714-df32-50d4-a3a7-992d2e6e61f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=8n-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538436} {:answer/id #uuid "757055cf-e772-58da-a703-f43ec9a3c7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=4n-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538495} {:answer/id #uuid "809e7a62-ae33-582b-8668-82433fc6dda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=2n-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538527} {:answer/id #uuid "94a2410d-cad5-53c3-8faf-cf633aeba7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=4n-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538581} {:answer/id #uuid "d67c9c6d-7661-524e-8b60-4eeb9d1f1a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=4n+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94a2410d-cad5-53c3-8faf-cf633aeba7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=4n-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538581} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa1e5362-5987-509c-a24c-44644e0e29bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e16d4c55-4032-51af-b361-3c91c642bb36" :question/math-academy-id "q-51559" :question/problem "The $8$th and $9$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $17$ and $21,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{8}=17$ and ${a}_{9}=21$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{9}-{a}_{8} \\\\  & =21-17 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{8}=17,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{8}$ is $7$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & ={a}_{1}+7d \\\\ 17 & ={a}_{1}+7(4) \\\\ 17 & ={a}_{1}+28 \\\\ 17-28 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-11+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-11+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97cdef9-0f97-5f2e-a168-2ea62e08a7f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f2e8d6-a320-5c71-a940-6eff81a662a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104843} {:answer/id #uuid "b10bb3d5-3989-5a9a-9609-1f63dd4288f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104844} {:answer/id #uuid "b15285ce-4b16-546d-9b37-88a677179181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104845} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed671c3-dab9-5786-babf-07b4b37801c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104846} {:answer/id #uuid "58a2c294-53aa-5fbd-a5be-1e2c3ca637a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b15285ce-4b16-546d-9b37-88a677179181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104845} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a126a1-4e90-5422-81e4-07bfbb4a953b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1bd11c6-80cb-5cfe-a97d-33176cbe000e" :question/math-academy-id "q-5435" :question/problem "Find the $31$st term of an arithmetic sequence given that the $22$nd term is $76$ and the $6$th term is $-4.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{22}=76$ and ${a}_{6}=-4$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{6}}{22-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{76-(-4)}{22-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{16} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{6}=-4,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{6}$ is $5$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}+5d \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}+5\\cdot 5 \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}+25 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-29+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-34+5n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $31$st term by substituting $n=31$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31} & =-34+5(31) \\\\  & =-34+155 \\\\  & =121\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
